175
00:16:35,398 --> 00:16:46,743
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as we already said, the successful practice of not taking things personally cannot be done unless behavior has been developed beforehand.

176
00:16:46,843 --> 00:16:58,487
[SPEAKER_03]: And by that, I mean, unless you have sufficiently not acted out of sensual desire, ill-will, irritation, ignorance, boredom, indolence, distraction, everything else that's unwholesome.

177
00:16:58,988 --> 00:17:01,088
[SPEAKER_03]: And all of those are choices that you make at a time.

178
00:17:01,769 --> 00:17:04,670
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you act towards them, it's because you chose to ignore

179
00:17:06,486 --> 00:17:07,787
[SPEAKER_03]: How not to take things personally?

180
00:17:15,171 --> 00:17:18,012
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be somewhat correct then to say that, eh...

181
00:17:20,153 --> 00:17:23,435
[SPEAKER_01]: This is why I've always... To be honest, I've always found this a kind of a...

182
00:17:23,715 --> 00:17:29,318
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to find it not really a useful advice, don't take it personally, because I'd be just thinking, I can't help it.

183
00:17:29,358 --> 00:17:30,619
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't help taking it personally.

184
00:17:30,639 --> 00:17:31,859
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a choice that I can make.

185
00:17:32,539 --> 00:17:33,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is true.

186
00:17:34,063 --> 00:17:47,614
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can kind of recognize that there are choices you can make that will, like, immediately result in taking things personally much less, and then further choices that will then become sort of available.

187
00:17:49,115 --> 00:17:56,481
[SPEAKER_03]: But even within, like, say, the overall picture, yeah, you can't just not take things personally, but part of you

188
00:17:57,362 --> 00:18:00,545
[SPEAKER_03]: Understands to the degree a possibility... That would be...

189
00:18:00,785 --> 00:18:02,327
[SPEAKER_03]: If I wouldn't take things personally.

190
00:18:02,367 --> 00:18:03,448
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, I'll just...

191
00:18:04,128 --> 00:18:05,089
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't want to think about it.

192
00:18:05,109 --> 00:18:06,530
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll carry on doing what I'm doing.

193
00:18:07,171 --> 00:18:14,297
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you stop and think about it, my point was that even colloquially, untrained mind would have a threat of recognition for what it is.

194
00:18:14,657 --> 00:18:18,801
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, if somebody says, oh, just relax.

195
00:18:18,901 --> 00:18:19,261
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

196
00:18:19,301 --> 00:18:20,883
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, relax your body.

197
00:18:22,512 --> 00:18:25,193
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you don't need... you don't need to be educated for that.

198
00:18:25,673 --> 00:18:31,236
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't need to learn any special techniques or methods, or yoga, nothing.

199
00:18:31,496 --> 00:18:40,220
[SPEAKER_03]: You would know internally, to a degree, you would have the attitude of relaxation, of certain letting go, to a degree.

200
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:41,961
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, the fact that you can't sustain that,

201
00:18:42,977 --> 00:18:51,241
[SPEAKER_03]: Longer is because everything else that you do and choose and the responsibility that you avoid and so on, you're completely, constantly disturbing it.

202
00:18:51,861 --> 00:18:58,544
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then you can keep saying, relax, relax, relax, but five minutes before that, you were acting out of desire and shouting at somebody out of anger.

203
00:18:59,224 --> 00:18:59,965
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's like, well,

204
00:19:01,165 --> 00:19:02,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying one thing, doing the other.

205
00:19:02,666 --> 00:19:03,147
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

206
00:19:03,187 --> 00:19:04,367
[SPEAKER_03]: Saying one thing, doing the other.

207
00:19:04,467 --> 00:19:07,069
[SPEAKER_03]: Thinking one thing, and then saying the other, and doing the other.

208
00:19:08,270 --> 00:19:20,358
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but if you actually stop and think about it, you realize there is already a recognition of that, just how to relax, and there is already a recognition of how to not take it personally.

209
00:19:21,048 --> 00:19:31,394
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you could, when the mind is balanced, say, you're not affected right there and then with ill-will, with anger, with sensuality, with distraction, with sleepiness, you're sort of just balanced.

210
00:19:33,275 --> 00:19:36,736
[SPEAKER_03]: And that experience, if you start thinking about it, can I not take this personally?

211
00:19:37,017 --> 00:19:38,858
[SPEAKER_03]: You would have some sense.

212
00:19:39,178 --> 00:19:40,839
[SPEAKER_03]: You would have a degree of understanding there.

213
00:19:42,179 --> 00:19:46,462
[SPEAKER_03]: And then see, why cannot I extend this attitude throughout my day?

214
00:19:47,102 --> 00:19:48,723
[SPEAKER_03]: Regardless of what feelings come up,

215
00:19:49,843 --> 00:19:55,730
[SPEAKER_03]: But how others act around me, cannot remain in personal point of view.

216
00:19:58,033 --> 00:20:02,058
[SPEAKER_03]: And there was that other sutra, it says the foremost of the outsider's views.

217
00:20:04,244 --> 00:20:15,071
[SPEAKER_03]: It boils down to exactly, like, basically, there is no person either here, but seen in that here-and-now, present manner, not on the level of some sort of explanation.

218
00:20:15,332 --> 00:20:21,396
[SPEAKER_03]: They would then say, well, nothing matters, because... And that is how most people would take, like, Buddhism in a traditional sense.

219
00:20:21,736 --> 00:20:25,118
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, anatta, see, nothing, everything is constantly changing, nothing is really ours.

220
00:20:25,879 --> 00:20:27,400
[SPEAKER_03]: But that is very much yours.

221
00:20:28,020 --> 00:20:30,302
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if somebody upsets you, you would feel it.

222
00:20:30,462 --> 00:20:32,003
[SPEAKER_03]: If somebody provokes you, you would feel it.

223
00:20:32,743 --> 00:20:34,483
[SPEAKER_03]: If somebody distracts you, you would act out of it.

224
00:20:34,503 --> 00:20:35,924
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're a thoroughly person.

225
00:20:35,944 --> 00:20:44,806
[SPEAKER_03]: You're just having this pasting, kind of, on top of a wound, that's, you know, putting a sticker that says, not person, but it's very much, in fact, a person.

226
00:20:49,247 --> 00:20:55,829
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why anatta can be understood only on the basis of fully understanding anicca and dukkha.

227
00:20:56,249 --> 00:20:57,449
[SPEAKER_03]: Anatta is the result of it.

228
00:20:58,549 --> 00:21:01,490
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yes, anicca and dukkha, discomfort,

229
00:21:02,439 --> 00:21:08,203
[SPEAKER_03]: Changeability, un-ability, impermanence, however you want to translate it, you recognize those are things here and now.

230
00:21:09,744 --> 00:21:12,045
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't matter the conditions, doesn't matter other people.

231
00:21:13,306 --> 00:21:26,054
[SPEAKER_03]: See, the fact that if you are not ignoring the fact that things are subject to change, even while they're permanent, permanently enduring here, there is a possibility of their change at any given time.

232
00:21:26,915 --> 00:21:31,498
[SPEAKER_03]: That's already going to prevent you from taking it personally, to the extent you would otherwise.

233
00:21:32,710 --> 00:21:39,832
[SPEAKER_03]: So, forgetting about anicca is another symptom of having being absorbed and taking things personally.

234
00:21:41,352 --> 00:21:48,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Forgetting that things, because they're subject to change at any given time, thus, they are uncomfortable.

235
00:21:49,375 --> 00:21:51,255
[SPEAKER_03]: To that extent, they are unpleasant.

236
00:21:53,716 --> 00:21:58,057
[SPEAKER_03]: Forgetting that discomfort is, again, going to make you take things personally.

237
00:21:58,777 --> 00:22:01,238
[SPEAKER_03]: Not forgetting the implicit discomfort of change,

238
00:22:04,016 --> 00:22:05,596
[SPEAKER_03]: You already have a better perspective, yeah.

239
00:22:05,636 --> 00:22:10,257
[SPEAKER_03]: So, anatta is the result of the right practice of right understanding of anicca and dukkha.

240
00:22:16,899 --> 00:22:19,960
[SPEAKER_02]: We're keeping that context alive, not forgetting it.

241
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:24,721
[SPEAKER_02]: The result of it is you are not touching anymore.

242
00:22:25,141 --> 00:22:25,741
[SPEAKER_02]: You're letting go.

243
00:22:25,981 --> 00:22:26,181
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

244
00:22:27,201 --> 00:22:28,441
[SPEAKER_03]: How would you keep the context alive?

245
00:22:30,422 --> 00:22:30,842
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the

246
00:22:32,060 --> 00:22:40,127
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the suitable fuel for keeping that context abandoned?

247
00:22:40,627 --> 00:22:44,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Aside from sense-restraint, those... Shame of undoing.

248
00:22:45,391 --> 00:22:47,112
[SPEAKER_03]: Shame of undoing, sense-restraint.

249
00:22:47,673 --> 00:22:56,940
[SPEAKER_03]: More immediately, what would be the... what's the immediate fuel for taking things personally or not?

250
00:22:57,901 --> 00:22:58,341
[SPEAKER_03]: What did you say?

251
00:22:58,722 --> 00:22:59,182
[SPEAKER_03]: Reflection.

252
00:23:02,460 --> 00:23:03,400
[SPEAKER_03]: What is pain?

253
00:23:04,141 --> 00:23:05,281
[SPEAKER_03]: Feeling.

254
00:23:05,421 --> 00:23:11,125
[SPEAKER_03]: So, whatever you're currently feeling, is, as I said, is the conscious point, is your place.

255
00:23:11,145 --> 00:23:19,070
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it doesn't matter the mess of confusion you might feel or think about on account of this or that.

256
00:23:19,987 --> 00:23:25,350
[SPEAKER_03]: The point of convergence is how it is being felt, in its broadest terms.

257
00:23:26,290 --> 00:23:31,333
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't take that personally, and you have created a space between you and the entire pressure.

258
00:23:31,633 --> 00:23:34,675
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how do you keep the context of anicca and dukkha?

259
00:23:34,755 --> 00:23:35,375
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

260
00:23:35,396 --> 00:23:42,439
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can... to basically constantly recollect and not lose sight of what you're currently feeling.

261
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:44,260
[SPEAKER_03]: What feeling is currently enduring?

262
00:23:44,961 --> 00:23:48,303
[SPEAKER_02]: I was thinking, because that's another sutta, remember?

263
00:23:54,027 --> 00:23:55,528
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you know you're taking things personally?

264
00:23:55,548 --> 00:23:56,548
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because you're pressured.

265
00:23:57,388 --> 00:23:58,269
[SPEAKER_02]: You are contacted.

266
00:23:58,829 --> 00:23:59,469
[SPEAKER_02]: You are pressured.

267
00:23:59,489 --> 00:24:00,289
[SPEAKER_02]: You are affected.

268
00:24:01,910 --> 00:24:02,770
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yes, you can know.

269
00:24:02,890 --> 00:24:03,170
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

270
00:24:03,630 --> 00:24:05,751
[SPEAKER_02]: And how is it that you feel that?

271
00:24:05,991 --> 00:24:06,171
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

272
00:24:06,211 --> 00:24:06,691
[SPEAKER_02]: You feel that.

273
00:24:06,711 --> 00:24:06,991
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

274
00:24:07,231 --> 00:24:11,713
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the convergence point, feeling.

275
00:24:12,053 --> 00:24:12,273
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

276
00:24:12,453 --> 00:24:16,894
[SPEAKER_03]: If you feel... To a degree, there is a feeling.

277
00:24:16,974 --> 00:24:19,455
[SPEAKER_03]: To that degree, there is some conceptual sense of I am.

278
00:24:19,795 --> 00:24:20,275
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why I don't

279
00:24:23,735 --> 00:24:24,375
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, what is it?

280
00:24:24,395 --> 00:24:26,296
[SPEAKER_03]: Because there are no traces of personality left.

281
00:24:26,696 --> 00:24:31,818
[SPEAKER_02]: Pressures pressure one who is attached or one who is holding.

282
00:24:32,679 --> 00:24:36,640
[SPEAKER_02]: How could pressures pressure one who is...

283
00:24:36,741 --> 00:24:37,641
[SPEAKER_03]: The groundless one.

284
00:24:37,901 --> 00:24:41,442
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, the groundless body, you know, who's not...

285
00:24:42,243 --> 00:24:44,444
[SPEAKER_03]: But there is no basis for anything to be pressured, yeah.

286
00:24:46,004 --> 00:24:51,887
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so that's the... like you don't start as an arahant, as I said, so you're going to have some degree of pressure, some degree of feeling.

287
00:24:52,803 --> 00:25:14,939
[SPEAKER_03]: But, through keeping perspective in regard to what you're feeling, that will already, well, make your sense of strength easier, certainly be freeing you from remorse of wrongdoing, and also, by remaining aware of what current feeling is enduring here and now, to that extent, you are not absorbed in it.

288
00:25:15,770 --> 00:25:19,031
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is the... that is the way of not taking it personally.

289
00:25:19,071 --> 00:25:20,991
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you might think like, oh, I shall...

290
00:25:21,672 --> 00:25:30,494
[SPEAKER_03]: I shall keep this context of not taking things personally alive by constantly reminding and thinking about it and reading suttas and stuff, but that will kind of wither away.

291
00:25:31,114 --> 00:25:32,875
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, inspiration comes and goes.

292
00:25:32,895 --> 00:25:34,095
[SPEAKER_03]: That's just how these things work.

293
00:25:34,915 --> 00:25:36,996
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you find things like

294
00:25:38,967 --> 00:26:05,236
[SPEAKER_03]: Pressures, feeling, even your current body posture, state, nature of it, what it's subjected to, all those things are on the level of here and now, and all of those things can stand as that convergent point from which you can remain impersonal towards it, to a degree at least, and then cultivate that further by not making it worse through unwholesome and unskillful actions.

295
00:26:05,945 --> 00:26:09,647
[SPEAKER_03]: Or you can ignore it and then get absorbed in those in those sense-objects.

296
00:26:12,088 --> 00:26:28,355
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly why a noble disciple in the suttas, the one who is committed to the gradual training, the Buddha encourages him to constantly remind and contemplate, not mine, not for me, not I am, in regard to everything that comes their way.

297
00:26:30,954 --> 00:26:39,621
[SPEAKER_03]: And, as we said already, that will have a practical result, because they are developed in body, speech and mind.

298
00:26:39,681 --> 00:26:42,203
[SPEAKER_03]: They're committed to the to the taming of the mind.

299
00:26:43,845 --> 00:26:56,315
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a question regarding sense restraint, especially for those on the, you know, gradual path, where, you know, often, especially at the beginning,

300
00:26:59,210 --> 00:27:09,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I guess I see a tendency toward virginity, and toward taking sense restraint personally.

301
00:27:09,513 --> 00:27:23,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially, I see a lot of young men with maybe aggressive mentalities coming to the monastery, and they want to do the dhūtanghas, and they want to... you know, all of these things.

302
00:27:25,565 --> 00:27:27,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't... it's not sustainable for them.

303
00:27:27,707 --> 00:27:28,127
[SPEAKER_00]: They leave.

304
00:27:28,648 --> 00:27:29,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

305
00:27:30,629 --> 00:27:40,317
[SPEAKER_00]: How can one have an attitude that doesn't... that's sustainable, that doesn't lead there, and that's not based in rigidity and taking personally of

306
00:27:41,213 --> 00:27:43,734
[SPEAKER_00]: I am someone who is restrained.

307
00:27:45,314 --> 00:27:48,035
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, first and foremost, is to learn what actual sense-restraint is.

308
00:27:48,095 --> 00:27:49,835
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the tangas are not sense-restraint.

309
00:27:51,336 --> 00:27:52,556
[SPEAKER_03]: Precepts are sense-restraint.

310
00:27:53,296 --> 00:28:00,898
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you don't need to be not laying down and eating out of your bowl and wearing rag ropes.

311
00:28:01,258 --> 00:28:06,060
[SPEAKER_03]: But you need to not tolerate thoughts of sensuality, even when you're sitting in a comfortable chair.

312
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:10,001
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if you're... even for a lay person who is not

313
00:28:11,608 --> 00:28:17,350
[SPEAKER_03]: Who is not capable to take on these dhūtanghas and find such an environment, you realize, well, it has nothing to do with the practice.

314
00:28:18,691 --> 00:28:21,272
[SPEAKER_03]: None of those ascetic practices have anything to do with the practice.

315
00:28:21,912 --> 00:28:27,735
[SPEAKER_03]: If they do, in a positive manner, it's for those who already understood what a practice is.

316
00:28:29,295 --> 00:28:34,917
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, they would see a direct relationship between those dhūtanghas and improvement in their practice.

317
00:28:35,958 --> 00:28:38,759
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all those young men need to learn, basically, what the dhamma is.

318
00:28:40,322 --> 00:28:40,702
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

319
00:28:41,343 --> 00:28:53,630
[SPEAKER_03]: They come there, they're inspired, they try to just, like, overpower everything, but it's like... but that's... that's like, you know, you get a guitar that's too loose, somebody tells you, you need to tighten the strings, and you smash the guitar.

320
00:28:55,371 --> 00:28:57,492
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the attitude.

321
00:28:57,632 --> 00:29:04,416
[SPEAKER_03]: So, initially, there will be some rigidity, even when it comes to just a sensory strain, for somebody who is very unrestrained.

322
00:29:05,677 --> 00:29:09,559
[SPEAKER_03]: But, again, by learning what the precepts are, where the line is,

323
00:29:10,943 --> 00:29:13,865
[SPEAKER_03]: You can then relax within that without becoming too loose.

324
00:29:15,906 --> 00:29:23,270
[SPEAKER_03]: Because initially, see, if you take rigidity to be a problem in and of itself, any form of saying no to yourself is a form of rigidity to that extent.

325
00:29:24,411 --> 00:29:27,993
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's, oh, but I must not be rigid, will then practically result go back to sensuality.

326
00:29:28,413 --> 00:29:33,616
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, no, I must have been rigid, correct, but I must not go back to sensuality either.

327
00:29:33,996 --> 00:29:34,857
[SPEAKER_03]: That's also correct.

328
00:29:35,712 --> 00:29:45,160
[SPEAKER_03]: So, until that balance is found, there's going to be some rigidity involved, even with basic sense restraint and five precepts, eight precepts, not even going into the tangas.

329
00:29:50,045 --> 00:29:56,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a role for joyful practice in leaning to that kind of restraint?

330
00:29:57,171 --> 00:30:01,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And I guess I'm asking, like, as an alternative to willpower, how can we

331
00:30:03,384 --> 00:30:06,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Cultivate a mind that tends toward restraint.

332
00:30:07,729 --> 00:30:08,590
[SPEAKER_03]: Through sense restraint.

333
00:30:09,171 --> 00:30:09,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

334
00:30:09,611 --> 00:30:09,812
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

335
00:30:10,532 --> 00:30:22,546
[SPEAKER_03]: That's like, as the suttas would describe often, only after you've been withdrawn and learned and understood the benefit of it, then the mind will naturally incline towards seclusion, non-sensuality.

336
00:30:23,925 --> 00:30:30,852
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no sort of a sweet pill you can take in the beginning, before that understanding.

337
00:30:30,932 --> 00:30:33,835
[SPEAKER_03]: But that understanding can only come to actually doing it first.

338
00:30:35,296 --> 00:30:41,322
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why the similes in the suttas were always about, you know, trapping a wild elephant and then

339
00:30:42,820 --> 00:30:52,063
[SPEAKER_03]: knocking the stakes in the ground, leather straps, and then it's it's it's urinating, defecating, screaming, fighting against it, and then you just have to wait until it comes down.

340
00:30:53,043 --> 00:30:57,065
[SPEAKER_03]: And it comes down, then you offer a bit of food, refuses, okay, you wait.

341
00:30:57,465 --> 00:31:02,507
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, okay, it takes the food, starts to recognize you, starts to listen, that's the mind.

342
00:31:02,807 --> 00:31:03,007
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

343
00:31:04,527 --> 00:31:11,810
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you still kind of have a desire for finding a way of joyful practice,

344
00:31:12,774 --> 00:31:15,916
[SPEAKER_03]: Probably a useful thing would be to understand why.

345
00:31:16,416 --> 00:31:17,697
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would that be important?

346
00:31:18,537 --> 00:31:22,860
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would joyful practice be important at this stage?

347
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:33,126
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, say, somebody gives you a medicine that's really bitter and makes your whole body rigid for a while, but you factually know and you can reason yourself that it is a beneficial medicine.

348
00:31:35,132 --> 00:31:37,294
[SPEAKER_03]: But I won't take it unless I can take it with sugar.

349
00:31:37,394 --> 00:31:39,977
[SPEAKER_03]: But you can't because sugar cancels the effects of the medicine.

350
00:31:40,958 --> 00:31:41,678
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the problem?

351
00:31:41,839 --> 00:31:44,161
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the problem with this?

352
00:31:44,181 --> 00:31:48,204
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is this so unacceptable to me that I need to make you joyful?

353
00:31:49,205 --> 00:31:52,188
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if I actually tame my mind, then everything will be joyful.

354
00:31:53,890 --> 00:31:54,430
[SPEAKER_03]: Effortlessly.

355
00:31:54,991 --> 00:31:55,871
[SPEAKER_03]: I will need to make it.

356
00:31:56,152 --> 00:31:58,214
[SPEAKER_03]: It will be the result of successful withdrawal.

357
00:31:59,062 --> 00:32:02,764
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, jhāna, the whole practice of it, is exactly that.

358
00:32:03,264 --> 00:32:15,690
[SPEAKER_03]: You finally feel, thoroughly, in like the deepest parts of your being, you feel free from debt, from peril, from threat, from anything that can harm you.

359
00:32:16,350 --> 00:32:19,352
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the result of withdrawal from the five hindrances.

360
00:32:19,872 --> 00:32:23,354
[SPEAKER_03]: They were the result of withdrawal from unwholesome states by body, speech and mind.

361
00:32:24,635 --> 00:32:36,931
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, joyfulness and joy, it should be cultivated, but kind of in its own time, when it comes as a result of withdrawal, not before.

362
00:32:37,892 --> 00:32:39,254
[SPEAKER_03]: And it sort of mirrors similar

363
00:32:40,142 --> 00:32:40,823
[SPEAKER_03]: Same with jhāna.

364
00:32:40,843 --> 00:32:45,265
[SPEAKER_03]: Like when the Buddha said you should cultivate jhāna, you should cultivate the pleasure that comes as a result of jhāna.

365
00:32:45,606 --> 00:32:47,227
[SPEAKER_03]: So, people want to start a practice with that.

366
00:32:48,167 --> 00:32:58,995
[SPEAKER_03]: I want the pleasure of jhāna to overwhelm me, make me feel great, and then all the hard work of restraining sense desire, will fall away, because I got the jhāna.

367
00:32:59,515 --> 00:33:01,736
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, actually, I tend to suit this closely.

368
00:33:01,756 --> 00:33:04,598
[SPEAKER_03]: It's always... no, that's the result of undoing all of this.

369
00:33:05,819 --> 00:33:06,600
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah.

370
00:33:09,012 --> 00:33:09,693
[SPEAKER_02]: Generosity.

371
00:33:10,253 --> 00:33:14,737
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, one of the first things is being generous, because actually, as you said...

372
00:33:14,857 --> 00:33:18,459
[SPEAKER_03]: Being virtuous in the basic sense, being generous... That's already...

373
00:33:18,520 --> 00:33:23,844
[SPEAKER_01]: It will make it easy, but it will be less, actually, less hard to then continue to do this.

374
00:33:23,924 --> 00:33:25,545
[SPEAKER_03]: And you can be recollecting those things.

375
00:33:25,565 --> 00:33:30,129
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no, there's no harm in doing that, even for, like, to uplifting of your own moods, factually.

376
00:33:30,729 --> 00:33:35,633
[SPEAKER_03]: Like when, when the disciple recollects his unbroken virtue, his generosity,

377
00:33:36,589 --> 00:33:41,992
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, oh yeah, the mind will be more joyful on account of that.

378
00:33:42,892 --> 00:33:49,635
[SPEAKER_03]: But, as long as joy is then experienced on account of those right reflections, that's fine.

379
00:33:50,155 --> 00:33:56,658
[SPEAKER_03]: Not if it's experienced on account of compromising the initially rigid state of precepts, or sense restraint.

380
00:33:57,819 --> 00:33:59,420
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, that's not the wholesome joy.

381
00:34:02,121 --> 00:34:04,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I say, that the main thing

382
00:34:05,495 --> 00:34:15,940
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to what you said, it's basically what you were saying about that you need to say no to yourself, to some things, but not to everything.

383
00:34:16,400 --> 00:34:17,760
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like one sentence.

384
00:34:18,081 --> 00:34:20,121
[SPEAKER_01]: You think, oh, yeah, yeah, that sounds... makes sense.

385
00:34:20,802 --> 00:34:29,265
[SPEAKER_01]: But in practice, it's really something that... it takes quite a bit of work to understand what you need to say no to.

386
00:34:30,006 --> 00:34:30,286
[SPEAKER_01]: And...

387
00:34:31,109 --> 00:34:41,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you need to kind of realize that it is something that needs to be understood, and you just don't just kind of take for granted the things to say no to, apart from the precepts.

388
00:34:41,072 --> 00:34:43,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the precepts of, you know, these basic things.

389
00:34:43,933 --> 00:34:55,616
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, on top of that, whatever is rooted in in greed, in aversion, in delusion, in unwholesome intentions, is kind of a... it's not something that you already start.

390
00:34:55,736 --> 00:34:59,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You start with some understanding of that, but then you still need to develop

391
00:34:59,560 --> 00:35:03,403
[SPEAKER_01]: understanding of what that means and kind of... Yeah, how deep those principles go.

392
00:35:03,503 --> 00:35:22,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, how deep it goes, how far it goes, to what extent, you know, on the level of thoughts, of speech or whatever, like small things that seem fine, but you... but then... and then also not allow yourself to verge into, like, yeah, like very easy then to kind of

393
00:35:22,679 --> 00:35:24,720
[SPEAKER_01]: That's quite a... That's quite a... That's quite a...

394
00:35:34,441 --> 00:35:37,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but basically, to find that line is to develop the right view.

395
00:35:37,864 --> 00:35:51,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we've kind of to realize that, okay, it's normal that it shouldn't be clear in the beginning, that I have to work to understand it, because if I fully develop understanding of you in this basic principle, that's that's developing the right view, like essentially becoming a sotāpanna.

396
00:35:51,458 --> 00:35:57,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's, yeah, just to kind of realize that it's not a trivial thing will already make you do it better.

397
00:35:59,146 --> 00:36:05,789
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, measure the task rightly, what needs to be done, recognize the extent of it, yeah, that's closer to success.

398
00:36:07,949 --> 00:36:09,830
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that is the, the suttas also describe that.

399
00:36:10,470 --> 00:36:16,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Sotāpanna is the one who understands wholesome is wholesome, good is good, and bad is bad.

400
00:36:17,685 --> 00:36:24,194
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means nobody who is not a sotāpanna does not fully understand the depth of what makes good good and bad bad.

401
00:36:24,976 --> 00:36:33,388
[SPEAKER_03]: Everybody can have an idea about it, but fully understanding that principle, seeing it for yourself, what is beneficial, what is unbeneficial, that's the sotāpanna, yeah?

402
00:36:35,168 --> 00:36:36,329
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the middle way, basically.

403
00:36:36,429 --> 00:36:36,669
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

404
00:36:36,769 --> 00:36:36,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

405
00:36:37,269 --> 00:36:38,250
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not the middle way yet.

406
00:36:38,270 --> 00:36:38,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

407
00:36:38,450 --> 00:36:40,111
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's sensuality on the wrong side.

408
00:36:40,351 --> 00:36:40,532
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

409
00:36:40,652 --> 00:36:48,477
[SPEAKER_03]: The reason I brought it up, because, yeah, because you all said it, because, like, it can be like, okay, yeah, so the precepts, the sense of strain, I do good, I don't do bad.

410
00:36:50,799 --> 00:36:52,020
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the practice.

411
00:36:52,560 --> 00:36:55,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Doing that and then fully understanding the principle of that, that is the practice.

412
00:36:56,143 --> 00:37:00,046
[SPEAKER_03]: That is exactly knowing what's... what leads to enlightenment and what doesn't.

413
00:37:00,466 --> 00:37:00,666
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

414
00:37:00,986 --> 00:37:02,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Sīla, samādhi, pañña.

415
00:37:02,207 --> 00:37:02,387
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

416
00:37:02,948 --> 00:37:03,288
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

417
00:37:03,428 --> 00:37:10,073
[SPEAKER_03]: And... but people... people, by not seeing that, is how they would then go and jump into these dhūtanghas or super inspirational agendas and so on.

418
00:37:10,553 --> 00:37:13,996
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like not recognizing that that's the degree they have to stay on.

419
00:37:15,010 --> 00:37:19,552
[SPEAKER_03]: They just want, okay, so if I keep doing this and tighten, tighten, tighten, tighten, tighten, that will result.

420
00:37:20,092 --> 00:37:30,537
[SPEAKER_03]: And, one way or the other, whether people commit to meditation techniques that result in being fully absorbed in your bodily sensations or whatever else, or commit to to tanga and random observances, they do it for that same reason.

421
00:37:30,917 --> 00:37:34,178
[SPEAKER_03]: Impatient, wanting to avoid things being endured on the right level.

422
00:37:34,999 --> 00:37:35,659
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a pride.

423
00:37:37,377 --> 00:37:38,798
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that goes hand in hand.

424
00:37:39,039 --> 00:37:40,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Conceit, sense of I am, none of that.

425
00:37:41,321 --> 00:37:49,428
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that is actually being dissolved if you stay, say, on the level of the feeling where everything else converges.

426
00:37:49,548 --> 00:37:55,373
[SPEAKER_03]: And by that, I also mean you don't act out of it to prolong it, but you don't act out of it to get rid of it.

427
00:37:55,453 --> 00:37:56,814
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's not yours to begin with.

428
00:37:57,715 --> 00:37:57,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

429
00:37:58,056 --> 00:37:59,757
[SPEAKER_03]: Why jumping into tango or something?

430
00:37:59,777 --> 00:38:00,698
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the problem with this?

431
00:38:01,459 --> 00:38:02,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Are you engaging sensuality?

432
00:38:02,920 --> 00:38:03,141
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

433
00:38:03,481 --> 00:38:03,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Ill will?

434
00:38:04,001 --> 00:38:04,222
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

435
00:38:04,802 --> 00:38:06,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Restlessness, remorse?

436
00:38:06,304 --> 00:38:06,464
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

437
00:38:07,004 --> 00:38:07,925
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, well, then stop.

438
00:38:08,125 --> 00:38:08,566
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop there.

439
00:38:09,026 --> 00:38:17,054
[SPEAKER_03]: But it becomes uncomfortable to stay there, because you're that... you're... if you are staying at that level, you cannot take things personally.

440
00:38:17,094 --> 00:38:19,777
[SPEAKER_03]: And from a personality point of view, that feels unpleasant.

441
00:38:20,157 --> 00:38:21,018
[SPEAKER_03]: That feels threatening.

442
00:38:21,726 --> 00:38:39,284
[SPEAKER_03]: So, now you're staying with another layer of threat, that's dissolving your personal point of view, and that's kind of the sotāpatti, which is why the people who did not quite want, or were not quite ready to understand the Buddha's teaching, but they insisted that he explained it to them, when he did,

443
00:38:43,051 --> 00:38:54,003
[SPEAKER_03]: The suttas described, they would fall on the ground, pull their hair and beat their breast, in kind of, in despair, saying, oh, that will kill me, that will destroy me, that will cancel me completely, annihilate who I am.

444
00:38:54,023 --> 00:38:55,864
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, yeah, that's exactly what it will do.

445
00:38:57,226 --> 00:39:03,993
[SPEAKER_03]: But they don't see the liberation and joy of that, they just see a threat, because that is, for many, that will be the first kind of thing that arises.

446
00:39:04,852 --> 00:39:11,858
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why then people would just not want to stay on that level, which is not that hard to kind of discern, but hard to keep yourself at.

447
00:39:12,498 --> 00:39:20,184
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you would then either wanna, oh, just do these practices, or uphold these routines, or do these techniques or methods.

448
00:39:20,424 --> 00:39:23,567
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I don't have to stay on this level where I am self-aware.

449
00:39:24,653 --> 00:39:31,186
[SPEAKER_03]: And when that kind of uncertainty of the choices, the weight of the actions, convergence points, it's all felt pressuring you.

450
00:39:32,569 --> 00:39:34,572
[SPEAKER_03]: No, just tell me what to do, so that I don't think about it.

451
00:39:34,693 --> 00:39:35,995
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, how many times we heard that?

